## The benchmark and what rests on it

Taste-Bench (Pan et al., arXiv 2609.25804, September 2026; dataset `wenbopan/taste-bench`; code
github.com/wbopan/tastebench) has 502 two-option questions cut from agent trajectories at a
"decision fork". Engineering items (390) come from GPT-5.4 and GPT-5.5 runs on SWE-bench Pro;
research items (112) from METR's MALT transcripts. GPT-5.6 Sol, the leaderboard's top model, wrote
both options of every item knowing which was correct. A panel of four judges (Kimi K2.5, GPT-4.1,
Llama 4 Maverick, Mistral Large 3) removed items all four answered from the options alone, and
kept items only when all four confirmed the label from the full record. Each item is asked in
both orders and counts only if both are right (chance 25%). The headline Average is the mean of
the engineering and research accuracies.

Conclusions under test ([`claims.md`](claims.md)): the 14-model ranking and "the best model
answers only 59.7%"; detour forks are harder than parallel ones "in both domains", read as
recognising a mistake before the agent did; accuracy falls with the time horizon; taste is only
partly correlated with SWE-bench Verified; labels agree with humans 98.8%; taste can be distilled.
The leaderboard takes new models by pull request.

## What we found

1. **The label is predictable from the origin of the options (C1, L2).** In a parallel item the
   prefix is the passing attempt's history, so the correct option is that trajectory's own next
   step. In a detour item the prefix stops just before the agent takes the abandoned direction,
   so the *wrong* option is that trajectory's own next step and the correct one (the recovery)
   uses knowledge from after the failure. The paper states both facts (App. A.2, §3.2). We
   pre-registered the consequence: an option's word overlap with the last 3,000 characters of the
   prefix (`cont`) and the share of its content words found anywhere in the prefix (`ground`)
   should point to the right answer in parallel cells and away from it in detour cells. Both do.

2. **The published per-cell pattern is what that rule predicts (C2).** Detour engineering is the
   hardest cell for all 14 models, and 7 of 14 are below 50% *single-order* there even among
   parsed replies (Claude Opus 5 40.6%, task-level 95% CI [32.3, 48.9]; GPT-5.4 Mini 38.7%
   [30.4, 47.0]). For 8 models more detour-engineering items are wrong in both orders than right
   in both. Below 50% single-order is not "cannot tell": these models consistently pick the option
   the prefix leads to. Without per-item published logs we cannot show item by item that they use
   the cue; the Haiku runs below test it for one small model.

3. **Other published statements that move.** Parse failures change ranks (C3); "detour harder
   in both domains" fails in research (C4); the paper's own repeat run of one model at one setting
   differs by more than most adjacent leaderboard gaps, and one research task carries up to 10.7
   points of weight in the Average (C5). Two plausible confounds were tested and the conclusion
   held: the horizon gradient is not cell composition (C6), and the SWE-bench comparison is not
   driven by the two models that built the items (C7).

| Quantity | n | Value | 95% CI or null |
|---|---|---|---|
| "Pick higher `cont`", parallel cells, full / held-out tasks | 172 / 88 | 64.0% / 65.9% | one-sided cluster-permutation p = 0.001 / 0.010 |
| "Pick higher `cont`", detour cells, full / held-out | 330 / 152 | 36.1% / 34.2% | p < 0.001 / 0.003 |
| "Pick higher `ground`", parallel / detour, held-out | 88 / 152 | 63.6% / 34.9% | p = 0.001 / 0.001 |
| Construction-aware `cont` floor: Average, full / held-out | 502 / 251 | 60.4 / 61.4 | best published 59.7 |
| Detour engineering, "pick lower `ground`", full / held-out | 266 / 135 | 61.7% / 63.0% | Holm p < 0.001; best published 48.1% |
| Models below 50% single-order on detour engineering (parsed) | 14 | 7 | 2 with task-level CI below 50 |
| Rubric-passing items all four judges solved from options alone | 1,809 | 1,080 (59.7%) | paper funnel |
| GPT-4.1 options-only on released parallel-engineering items | 124 | 63.7% | ≥ 4 of 14 models' full-context single-order accuracy |

Bias direction. The construction cue makes models that follow the prefix look good on parallel
forks and bad on detour forks. It therefore inflates the detour–parallel gap and the paper's
reading of it. Its effect on the overall ranking depends on how strongly each model follows the
prefix, which aggregate data cannot show.

## The confound

`cont` = Jaccard overlap of the sets of ≥ 3-character lower-case word tokens of an option and of
the last 3,000 characters of `prefix_text`. `ground` = share of the option's ≥ 4-character content
tokens that occur anywhere in the prefix. Rule: pick the option with the higher value; ties wrong.
Order-invariant, so its paired and single-order accuracies are equal. Definitions, signs and the
held-out split (tasks 50/50 within cell, seed 0) were committed before the data were downloaded
(`prereg.md`, amendment 1; `scripts/b1_features.py`). Per-cell results
([`results/b2_rules.json`](results/b2_rules.json)):

| Cell | "higher `cont`" | "higher `ground`" | items |
|---|---|---|---|
| Parallel engineering | 62.9% | 58.1% | 124 |
| Parallel research | 66.7% | 64.6% | 48 |
| Detour engineering | 35.3% (lower: 56.0%) | 33.1% (lower: 61.7%) | 266 |
| Detour research | 39.1% (lower: 59.4%) | 43.8% (lower: 46.9%) | 64 |

The other pre-registered form features (length, paths, code spans, identifiers, numbers,
verification and hedging words) do not reach the L2 bar in any cell after Holm correction. Length
is weakly informative in engineering (the longer option is right 61% of the time in both
engineering cells; full-set p = 0.03 and 0.006; held-out 55–56%). The answer letter is balanced
(A correct in 44–52% per cell).

Examples by id (no text is reproduced). Parallel items where the correct option names the file or
symbol the prefix was just reading: `par-sse-67dcb776a7cedb99-v2`, `par-sse-6cb45bffd8ff7b20-v2`.
Detour items where the wrong option repeats the agent's stated next plan in the prefix tail:
`det-sse-d8db8b8100b5d57d-v3`, `det-sse-a3b9e33520ba1b14-v3`.

What detour engineering is about (hand read of 13, flagged keyword scan of all 266): 12 of 13 sampled
items are forks between two ways to verify a patch or obtain a working tool in a sandbox with
missing dependencies; 220 of 266 queries contain verification or environment-failure vocabulary
and 164 of 266 ask the reader to choose or select the next step. The generator rewrote these
queries to pose the fork. Several labels depend on sandbox facts the prefix does not settle.

## The effect

Published per-item logs are not released (only per-cell aggregates in `results/*/summary.json`),
so the L3 test on published models is limited to cells.

| Cell | Rule pointing to the right answer | Model mean (14) | Models < 50% single-order | Best model |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Parallel engineering | `cont` 62.9% | 58.1 | 0 | 75.8 |
| Parallel research | `cont` 66.7% | 50.9 | 0 | 64.6 |
| Detour engineering | `cont` 35.3% | 35.9 | 7 | 48.1 |
| Detour research | `cont` 39.1% | 50.8 | 1 | 68.8 |

The ordering of cells by model accuracy follows the ordering by the rule in engineering, and the
research cells, where the cue is weaker in detour (`ground` 43.8%), show no detour penalty. Four
cells cannot establish the relation; the Haiku results are the item-level test.

HAIKU_SECTION

## Secondary results

**C3 Unparsed replies (L3).** All 14 leaderboard rows reproduce from the summaries (max
difference 0.05 points). The parser accepts only a final `ANSWER: X` line or a bare letter
(`src/tastebench/paired.py:80-87`). Recomputing among parsed replies (exact for single-order;
bounds for paired, because the summaries do not say which items had a failed presentation):

| Model | Unparsed / 1,004 | Published Average | Parsed-only Average (bounds) | Single-order, all / parsed |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| GLM-5.2 | 17 | 53.9 | 55.9–57.0 | 60.7 / 61.7 |
| GPT-5.4 Mini | 112 | 40.1 | 42.3–45.0 | 42.5 / 47.9 |
| Mistral Medium 3.5 | 24 | 37.7 | 39.9–41.5 | 53.5 / 54.8 |
| GPT-5.4 Nano | 93 | 36.6 | 40.7–43.9 | 46.6 / 51.4 |
| Grok 4.20 Reasoning | 459 | 15.7 | ≥ 32.8 | 34.5 / 63.5 |

GLM-5.2's lower bound passes Claude Opus 5, Grok 4.5 and GPT-5.6 Terra (rank 6 → 3). GPT-5.4 Nano
passes GPT-5.4 Mini and Mistral. Grok 4.20 Reasoning, published at 15.7 (below chance), answers
63.5% of the presentations it formats correctly, the same as GPT-5.6 Sol. The paper reports the
unparsed counts and drops three models from its SWE-bench comparison for this reason, so the
mechanism is disclosed; its effect on the ranking is not. Parse failure need not be random (a
model may fail to format on hard items), so parsed-only numbers are an upper reading.

**C4 "Detour harder than parallel in both domains" (body.tex:178).** Engineering: model-mean
−22.1 points, 14 of 14 models. Research: −0.1 points (50.8 vs 50.9, the paper's own appendix
numbers), 7 of 14 models; the item-level interval for the model-mean difference is about ±19
points. The two top models are 11–12 points *better* on research detours. The claim holds for
engineering only.

**C5 Precision (L3).** The paper ran GPT-5.6 Sol at xhigh twice: the main run (pooled both-orders
accuracy 58.2%, reproduced from Table 7 and from the summary) and the effort rerun (55.6%, Table
8). Same prompt, settings and items; 2.6 points (about 13 items) apart, and 5.4 points apart on the
219 "inferable" items. That run-to-run difference exceeds 8 of the 13 adjacent gaps on the
leaderboard. Clusters: parallel research has 48 items from 8 tasks, one task
(`ai_rd_fix_embedding/main`) supplies 17 of them, and the same task supplies 7 detour-research
items: 24 of 112 research items, 10.7 points of weight in the Average. A research item weighs 3.5
times an engineering item. The item-level 95% half-width of a model's Average is about ±5.0
points (±5.7 to ±6.8 with within-task correlation 0.1–0.3), larger than 12 of 13 adjacent gaps.
Paired comparisons between models are tighter, but no per-item data exist to compute them.

**C6 Horizon gradient (R).** If accuracy depended only on the cell, the cell mix of each horizon
level would predict 47.6, 43.6, 41.8 and 44.0 (in prefix → more work); observed 62.3, 42.9, 31.5,
21.0. Composition explains 3.6 of the 41.3-point gap. The gradient is within cells. Two caveats
remain untested: the annotator (GPT-5.5) saw the answer when judging how "visible" it was, and 10
of 14 models are below 25% on the 69 "more work" items, which under the paired rule means
consistent wrong choices rather than guessing.

**C7 SWE-bench comparison (R).** SWE-bench Verified values read from the figure's vector data
(`scripts/read_figures.py`; the Averages read back to within 0.04 points) reproduce the paper's
r = 0.63 (0.625) and engineering r = 0.37 (0.366). The largest positive residual is GPT-5.5
(+9.6), an engineering-trajectory author and the horizon annotator; GPT-5.6 Sol is +3.8. Dropping
both gives r = 0.687, the 69th percentile of the 55 drop-two values; dropping GPT-5.5 alone gives
0.733. The "partly correlated" conclusion does not depend on the construction models.

**Exploratory (flagged).** The two Claude models have the largest research-minus-engineering gaps
(+17.6, +14.6 against a median of +7.8; exact permutation p = 0.018 for the pair). Research
trajectories were written by Claude and DeepSeek agents. This was noticed before the prereg and
has two plain alternative readings (Claude better at ML research; Claude worse at detour
engineering). GPT models show no matching engineering advantage (p = 0.50).

**C8 Options-only signal (L2 from the paper's numbers).** 1,080 of 1,809 rubric-passing items
(59.7%) were answered correctly by all four judges from the options alone, between 51% (detour
engineering) and 69% (detour research). On released parallel-engineering items GPT-4.1 still
answers 63.7% from the options alone, in one shuffled order; GPT-5.4 Mini, Mistral Medium 3.5,
GPT-5.4 Nano and Grok 4.20 score at or below that with the full prefix, single-order. Llama 4
Maverick, at 34.7% on released items, is the judge whose errors kept most items in.

**C9 Labels and training claims (L1).**
- The human review's 98.8% is measured after reviewers saw summaries of the outcomes, and pools
  15 items the judge panel removed as label-inconsistent. All 172 retained judgments contain only
  2 disagreements, so if those 15 items had the overall retention (86%), humans agreed with the
  label on at least 92% of judgments on items the panel rejected. The statistic cannot detect the
  defect it is cited against. The first-stage (before outcome) human choices, which would be a
  human baseline, are not reported.
- 5 of 64 detour-research prefixes contain an agent's stated plan to sabotage or sandbag (keyword
  scan, a lower bound; ids in `results/b1_inventory.json`). MALT includes such runs by design.
- End-to-end: 14.6% and 39.0% are 6/41 and 16/41, but 33.7% (student advice) is not a whole-task
  rate of 41; it needs at least 5 runs per task or another denominator, which the paper does not
  state. There is no base-model-advice or random-advice control, and the advice text contains both
  candidates mined from earlier runs on the same task, so the gain cannot be attributed to the
  student's judgment.
- Distillation trains on labels for options written by the same generator as the test fold, so
  "transfers to unseen tasks" could be learning the generator's option style. Untested.

**Truncation (null).** With the harness's own preflight (o200k_base × 1.02), no item exceeds the
65,536-token cap (max 55,072, one detour-research item). Truncation cannot move any result.

## Robustness

| Check | Result | Claim survives? |
|---|---|---|
| Held-out tasks (direction fixed on half 1 or by prereg) | `cont` 65.9% / 34.2%; floor Average 61.4 | Yes |
| Second feature definition (`ground` instead of `cont`) | 63.6% / 34.9% held-out | Yes |
| Cluster-aware null (flip whole tasks, 1,000 draws) | p ≤ 0.01 for both constructions | Yes |
| Placebo feature (option position in published order) | answer letter A in 44–52% per cell | Not informative, as expected |
| Holm over 40 feature × cell tests | detour-engineering `ground` survives (p < 0.001) | Yes for that cell |
| By domain | cue strong in both engineering cells and parallel research; weak in detour research | Partly: detour research is the exception |
| A2 parsed-only bounds use the conservative end | GLM rank change holds at the lower bound | Yes |
| Retest difference vs adjacent gaps on Average scale | difference measured on pooled accuracy, gaps on Average | Approximate; the Average weights research items 3.5×, so the Average difference is likely larger |

## Alternative explanations

1. *The cue is the construct.* A good engineer at a detour fork should prefer the option that
   is not just "more of the same". Then a low-overlap rule would be taste. But the rule never
   reads the options' meaning, and in parallel forks the same rule flips sign: the
   higher-overlap option is right. A judgment that reverses with how the item was mined is
   tracking the mining.
2. *Detour items are genuinely harder.* Possible and not excluded. The research detour cell,
   where the cue is weak, shows no detour penalty, which fits the cue reading better; with 64
   items it is weak evidence.
3. *Prefix overlap is a proxy for difficulty or horizon.* The horizon annotation is per item
   but GPT-5.5 made it with the answer visible. Not tested against the cue.

## What was already known

The paper discloses the option origins, the generator, the judge panel and the funnel, the
unparsed counts, and the human review's design. No independent critique exists
([`prior_work.md`](prior_work.md)). New here: the origin cue and its opposite signs by
construction; the content-blind floor above the leaderboard; below-chance single-order
accuracy on detour engineering; parsed-only ranks; the paper's own test–retest pair against
the gaps; the cluster weights; the research-domain failure of "detour harder in both domains";
the human-review arithmetic; the MALT sabotage runs among items.

## What this does not show

- That published models use the cue. Per-item published logs do not exist. The cell-level
  pattern fits it; the Haiku results show one small model's behaviour only.
- That the labels are wrong. They are outcome labels for real runs; the point is what else they
  carry.
- Anything about the distillation result beyond the missing controls.

## Pins and how to reproduce

- Dataset `wenbopan/taste-bench` sha `069aa742…`; parquet sha256 engineering `377c6031…`,
  research `8528ca9f…`, manifest `ab2db9cc…` (checked in `scripts/b_common.py`). Gated: request
  access with your own account. Items are not reposted here; only ids, hashes, feature values and
  aggregates.
- Repo `wbopan/tastebench` at `b636e868`; paper source tarball sha256 `675cebfd…`.

```bash
cd audits/taste-bench
git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/wbopan/tastebench data/repo
mkdir -p data/paper/src && curl -sL https://arxiv.org/e-print/2609.25804 | tar -xz -C data/paper/src
# gated: download data/hf/{export_manifest.json,data/*/test-00000-of-00001.parquet} at the sha above
uv venv data/.venv && uv pip install --python data/.venv/bin/python numpy pandas pyarrow scipy tiktoken
P="data/.venv/bin/python -I"
$P scripts/read_figures.py; $P scripts/a_aggregate.py          # Part A (no gated data)
$P scripts/b1_features.py; $P scripts/b2_floor.py; $P scripts/b6_tokens.py
$P scripts/b4_haiku.py H0; $P scripts/b4_haiku.py H1; $P scripts/b5_score.py
$P scripts/make_results.py
```

Seeds 0; permutations 1,000; bootstraps 2,000. Every number above is in
[`results.json`](results.json).
